ield" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/state.json" "file_sha256" "858229e30cb7ce79c69cf63593b2b370c6c4e30e12b1c029d2cc7585441e5870" "basis" 1079 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7436.json" "file_sha256" "55ec1fc4f19347fcb9671666c8adb2d34d9f4efed717ac3a6445363831fc3bd9" "basis" 1079 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7436.json" "file_sha256" "55ec1fc4f19347fcb9671666c8adb2d34d9f4efed717ac3a6445363831fc3bd9" "basis" 1079 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7437.json" "file_sha256" "0a581faf5bc749dda96815b08d0335f3e472a9b5a67ab03aac03a6e0d1380749" "basis" 1079 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7437.json" "file_sha256" "0a581faf5bc749dda96815b08d0335f3e472a9b5a67ab03aac03a6e0d1380749" "basis" 1079 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7442.json" "file_sha256" "bac757502a1816be14619a87274292e55a45b7878f6b2789f6d498b476c6fa8f" "basis" 1079 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/example-7442.json" "file_sha256" "bac757502a1816be14619a87274292e55a45b7878f6b2789f6d498b476c6fa8f" "basis" 1079 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-2t,\\,y=-\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${x}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/state.json" "file_sha256" "8238d63beb175b191cd2c9e7e7c60e2c7509e576fa16b5608c447171651b11f0" "basis" 1208 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =-\\frac{3y}{4} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-3y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/state.json" "file_sha256" "8238d63beb175b191cd2c9e7e7c60e2c7509e576fa16b5608c447171651b11f0" "basis" 1208 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a67ea273ef0cb0349fa2737d67de87544f259bda79792a0d407c2cb6c1f45894" "basis" 1208 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/state.json" "file_sha256" "8238d63beb175b191cd2c9e7e7c60e2c7509e576fa16b5608c447171651b11f0" "basis" 1208 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/state.json" "file_sha256" "8238d63beb175b191cd2c9e7e7c60e2c7509e576fa16b5608c447171651b11f0" "basis" 1208 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-106412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/237e807e6a36b3a6b5ac92fc3fa93d046761170ac2558511020ce382f70fd3c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(6,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,-6),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ 3 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-3t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-84673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-6t"] ["math" "x=-6t,\\,\\,y=6{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-3t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-3t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/79609f38fef6121500dfc553137a6dd3af3a85ecd838a098c452748ab51ea13d.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-6,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-6,6),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6=2pt \\\\ 6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y=(\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-280209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280209" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280209" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}t^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/704181219623dc7269e7bcbb4de75c4181a5be4db3a097eb3d9da141dc64a403.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(10,5).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(10,5),$ we can substitute $x=10$ and $y=5$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}10=2pt \\\\ 5=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2pt \\\\ 5 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =p{(\\frac{5}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{25}{p} \\\\ p & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(5)t,\\,y=(5){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=10t,\\,y=5{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-280210" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280210" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280210" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280210" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5t^2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-7,-7)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-7,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-7$ and $y=-7$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7=2pt \\\\ -7=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{7}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =p{(-\\frac{7}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -7 & =p\\cdot \\frac{49}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -7 & =\\frac{49}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-7 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{7}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{7}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-7t,\\,\\,y=-14{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-7{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-7t"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-7{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{2}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=32x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =32x \\\\ 4p & =32 \\\\ p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(8){t}^{2},\\,y=2(8)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=8{t}^{2},\\,y=16t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-280226" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280226" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8t^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280226" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280226" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-274755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=24x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-274755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =24x \\\\ 4p & =24 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(6){t}^{2},\\,y=2(6)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=6{t}^{2},\\,y=12t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-274755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-274755" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-274755" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6t^2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-274755" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-274755" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=12x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =12x \\\\ 4p & =12 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=3t,\\,\\,y=6{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=3t"] ["math" "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t"] ["math" "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-106420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{4}{5}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{4}{5})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{8}{5}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280233" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{8}{5}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/c8ec60cf31b66610fd9ddc8fc3fb8c30e8a27d2de41b6ecdeb358afa60588cf4.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(8,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,2),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-84719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=8{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=16t"] ["math" "x=8{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=4t"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{8}t"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{16}t"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{2},\\,y=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{4},\\,t=\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=9x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-280337" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280337" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-2{t}^{2},\\,y=-4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-8x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-84734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{2}=-6x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=-8x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=8x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{2}=-8x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-2t,\\,y=-\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${x}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =-\\frac{3y}{4} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-3y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-280361" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2}{3}y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=6y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-84736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}=12y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=3y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=6y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{8}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}=6y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4x,\\,t=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8eacd9f95f85db451ab6e0a4441169a62d0ec1343722fca36ae44d4f6f48fc60"} {"question" "q-84731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{2}=2x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=4x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}x"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "q-84731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{2}=x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/state.json" "file_sha256" "5db26b44547fa113584559c81f29a438832e4e54ce8554324941d14176edcaba"} {"question" "e-7436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/example-7436.json" "file_sha256" "47f35d56ece35e477c328a2736ba9915da6e8c8145559773be10c5550ac99e1b"} {"question" "e-7436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/example-7436.json" "file_sha256" "47f35d56ece35e477c328a2736ba9915da6e8c8145559773be10c5550ac99e1b"} {"question" "e-7437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/example-7437.json" "file_sha256" "0a581faf5bc749dda96815b08d0335f3e472a9b5a67ab03aac03a6e0d1380749"} {"question" "e-7437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/example-7437.json" "file_sha256" "0a581faf5bc749dda96815b08d0335f3e472a9b5a67ab03aac03a6e0d1380749"} {"question" "e-7442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/example-7442.json" "file_sha256" "bac757502a1816be14619a87274292e55a45b7878f6b2789f6d498b476c6fa8f"} {"question" "e-7442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/example-7442.json" "file_sha256" "bac757502a1816be14619a87274292e55a45b7878f6b2789f6d498b476c6fa8f"}] "usage" {"q-280361" [] "q-106412" [] "q-84675" [] "e-7437" [] "e-7436" [] "e-7442" [] "q-280233" [] "q-84731" [] "q-106420" []} "basis" 1347 "retractions" [[17592186285790 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?"] [17592186285830 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186088379 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\frac{1}{4}t^{2}, y = \\frac{1}{2}t, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."] [17592186087567 :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186087205 17592186087206 17592186087207 17592186087208 17592186087209 17592186285792 17592186285793 17592186285794 17592186285795 17592186285796 17592186285799 17592186285800 17592186285801 17592186285802 17592186285803 17592186285832 17592186285833 17592186285835 17592186285849 17592186285850 17592186285851 17592186285852 17592186285853 17592186292942] "immutable_fields" [[17592186087204 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087204 :answer-field/id] [175921860872